  "narrative": "All 12 new balanced-predictor comparisons are valid and have large cell sizes, so the main scientific issue is not feasibility but whether the signed effect sizes and uncertainty intervals support a clinically meaningful association. For these direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means feature=1 has a more negative dependency score than feature=0, i.e., stronger dependency; a negative signed estimate means the opposite, weaker dependency. I used approximately 10% of the absolute outcome mean as the clinical benchmark: about 0.025 for outcome_013, 0.015, and 0.016, and about 0.009 for outcome_014, with extra caution for outcome_014 because its mean is near zero and a relative threshold is unstable there.\n\nH13 (outcome_013/feature_034) has a signed estimate of -0.0187, indicating the opposite of the claimed stronger-dependency direction. Its signed upper bound, 0.0012, is far below the clinical benchmark, so clinically meaningful stronger dependency is ruled out; H13 is rejected and closed. The same evidence suggests a possible weaker-dependency signal, so I registered a direction +1 refinement rather than accepting H13. H14 (outcome_013/feature_002) has a tiny positive estimate and an upper bound 0.0235 below the approximate benchmark, so I rejected it. H15 (outcome_013/feature_060) is not robustly positive, but its upper bound 0.0328 exceeds the benchmark; it is unresolved and deferred.\n\nFor outcome_014, H16-H18 all have near-zero point estimates and wide intervals. Because outcome_014's mean is close to zero, the 10% relative benchmark is numerically small and can be unstable; therefore I did not reject these solely because the point estimates are small, but I also did not accept them. They remain unresolved and deferred.\n\nFor outcome_015, H20 and H21 have small effects and upper confidence bounds well below the benchmark, so they are rejected and closed. H19 (outcome_015/feature_034) is the only new result whose interval entirely excludes zero: signed estimate 0.0170, interval [0.0017, 0.0323]. This supports stronger dependency for feature_034=1, but the point estimate is below the approximate 10% benchmark and the interval straddles the threshold. It is scientifically interesting but not sufficient for acceptance. I marked H19 active and request validation to assess whether the effect is stable and whether it might be clinically meaningful. H20 and H21 do not receive validation.\n\nFor outcome_016, H23 has a small negative point estimate and upper bound below the benchmark, so it is rejected. H22 and H24 also have signed upper bounds below the benchmark for the claimed stronger-dependency direction, so those original claims are rejected and closed. However, their lower signed bounds indicate the raw effect is on the opposite side, suggesting possible weaker-dependency associations. I registered direction +1 refinements for H22/feature_034 and H24/feature_060 to evaluate that opposite hypothesis cleanly. No claim is accepted based solely on these discovery results, and these are associations in this dataset only, not causal or generalizable effects.",
  "research_notes": "After iteration 2 appraise: H13-H24 delivered. Thresholds: outcome_013 ~0.0269, outcome_015 ~0.0250, outcome_016 ~0.0256; outcome_014 ~0.0093 but relative threshold unstable. Rejected/closed: H13, H14, H20, H21, H22, H23, H24. Deferred unresolved: H15, H16, H17, H18. Active unresolved: H19, validation requested. H19 is outcome_015/feature_034, direction -1, positive signed estimate 0.0170, CI [0.0017,0.0323], excludes zero but point below clinical benchmark and upper crosses benchmark. Register opposite-direction refinements for H13 outcome_013/feature_034 direction +1; H22 outcome_016/feature_034 direction +1; H24 outcome_016/feature_060 direction +1. These test weaker dependency suggested by negative signed estimates under direction -1. Next analyze stage: prioritize validation response for H19, then likely run H25-H36 for high-dispersion outcomes 001, 006, 008, 011 and/or the new opposite-direction refinements. Avoid further validation of H15-H18 unless new evidence suggests robustness. Keep causal and generalization claims out of scope. If H19 validation does not show clinically meaningful magnitude, reject H19 or keep closed after validation; if validation confirms larger effect, reassess acceptance.",
